"A machine-learning pipeline diagram connects data preparation, training, evaluation, and versioned model artifacts through explicit gates.\n\nUse when: Review reproducible model-building stages. Explain which evidence qualifies a trained model for registration.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is only prediction-time service placement.\n\nVisual grammar: Task nodes name preparation, training, evaluation, and registration behaviors. Artifact nodes identify versioned datasets, parameters, metrics, and models. Gate edges state acceptance conditions separately from observed results.\n\nBuild: Select one model family and state the pipeline boundary. Identify immutable inputs and the artifacts each task produces. Add evaluation gates and rejected-model paths. Arrange tasks in dependency order with artifacts in separate rows and clear edge corridors.\n\nCheck: Training and evaluation data have intentional separation. Metrics carry dataset, revision, and measured or target status. Registration follows the stated evaluation gate. Artifact titles and gate labels are clear of arrows and group borders.\n\nPitfalls: Treating successful training execution as proof of model suitability. Inventing accuracy numbers to make an illustrative pipeline look complete.\n\nExample: A classifier pipeline prepares examples, trains a candidate, evaluates a held-out set, then registers only qualifying artifacts.\n\nAlternatives: Feature store flow diagram, Orchestration DAG."
